%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D9F37BFE] # Analyzing Large Datasets
%[text] In this exercise, we use a subset of a classic dataset assembled by Francis Galton in the 1880s. Galton was a pioneer in the quantitative study of heredity and individual differences, and his work on these data contributed to the development of the statistical concepts of regression and correlation. He also coined the term *eugenics* and became a leading advocate of the eugenics movement.
%[text] Galton was interested in the relationship between parents' heights and the heights of their adult children. The subset used here contains measurements from 898 adult children and their parents, drawn from 205 families. His analysis was among the earliest attempts to study parent-offspring resemblance quantitatively. He also examined whether husbands' and wives' heights were associated with one another, an early investigation of what is now called assortative mating.
%[text] ## Data Analysis Workflow
%[text] When analyzing data, you usually go through the following steps:
%[text] 1. Load
%[text] 2. Review
%[text] 3. Clean-up any noise in the data
%[text] 4. Visualize
%[text] 5. Perform statistics
%[text] 6. Final Report
%[text] 7. Repeat as necessary \
%[text] So, let's do that...
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6822A2FD] ## Reset MATLAB
close all
clearvars
clc
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_60485269] ## Load the Data 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_91D1D70B] ### Get file path
%[text] The Galton data we will use is contained in a file called '05-1-galton-x.csv'. A .csv file is a plain-text spreadsheet file type. You can find the file in the `MtMresources/data/unit1` folder
%[text] To load the file, we need its file path. Remember, a file path is just a character array that MATLAB can read to find files.
%[text] #### Add a GUI widget
%[text] Instead of copying the paths of files, you have another option in live scripts. You can simply add a file browser control that creates the full path for you. 
%[text] Create the file browser control as follows. First, switch to the Live Editor tab. Then Search for the Control Button. If you hold the button down, you will get a pop-up Control Menu:
%[text] ![](text:image:3e3f)![](text:image:1480)
%[text] In the code block below
%[text] 1. Insert a file browser control. Name the output from the control: `csvpath` \
csvpath = []; % replace brackets with File Browser control
%[text] - you will have to run this code block and manually select the file from the MATLAB drive
%[text] - But once you have run this code, you won't have to run the block again. Even if you restart MATLAB
%[text] - The full path is saved in the code block.
%[text] - If you move the file to a different location on your computer, simply re-run this code to set its new location. \
%[text] Run this code block by clicking on the yellow folder icon.
%[text] - Remember: WE HAVEN'T OPENED THE FILE
%[text] - We just created the file path so MATLAB can find the file \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C1339E0E] ### Spreadsheet Import function
%[text] Now that we have the file path, we are ready to load the data. The function [getGaltonData](internal:M_8bd5) is a local function that loads the spreadsheet data into a table variable. I created this function using the **Import Data** tool. This function's input is the csv file path that you just created.
T = getGaltonData(csvpath)
%%
%[text] ## Review the Data 
%[text] You should now have a table, `T` in the workspace. Double-click on the variable to bring it up in the Variable Window.
%[text] Note that the [data is tidy](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/dataWrangling/tidyData/) and that there are Six Columns (Variables) in the table
%[text] -  **Family** - Family id (categorical data)
%[text] -  **Father** - Father's height in inches
%[text] -  **Mother** - Mother's height in inches
%[text] -  **Sex** - sex of child (categorical data)
%[text] -  **Height** - height of child (inches)
%[text] -  **Kids** - number of kids per family \
%[text] There are two grouping variables, Family and Sex. The rest of the variables contain the measurements made (height in inches or count)
%[text] Each row in the table is an observation. Notice how some data is repeated in this type of table. For example, lines 1-4 are data from one family. There is one row per child, with data pertinent to each child. The rest of the data repeats: Family ID, the father's and mother's height, and the number of kids per family.
%%
%[text] ### Grouping Categories
%[text] The two grouping variables have the following categories
%[text] - Sex has two categories ( `M` and `F`)
%[text] - Family has 197 categories (1 for each family) \
%[text] The function `categories` returns the categories in a categorical array
categories(T.Sex)
categories(T.Family)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Missing Data
%[text] Sometimes you don't have a value for a given observation. This is called missing data and might be entered as:
%[text] - `NaN` - not a number
%[text] - `<missing>` - common for string arrays
%[text] - `<undefined>` - common for categorical arrays \
%[text] There are other options too. But, we'll focus on the main ones. If data is missing, then we will have to take that into account when we are performing our statistics.
%[text]  We can check if our table has any missing data using the function `ismissing`
ismissing(T)
%[text] - this returns a logical array the size of the table
%[text] - Any logical 1's would indicate a missing data point \
%%
%[text] To summarize the logical array, just `sum`:
sum(ismissing(T))
%[text] - We have no missing data in our table. So, good.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8A748267] ## Visualize the data
%[text] The next step in the workflow is to visualize your data. This will give you a overview of how the data is distributed and help you identify:
%[text] - **Outliers** that strongly influence an analysis.
%[text] - **Nonlinear relationships** that a correlation or linear regression may miss.
%[text] - **Unequal variability** across groups or values.
%[text] - **Clusters, gaps, or subgroups** that suggest the data are not from a single homogeneous population.
%[text] - **Skewed or unusual distributions** that make standard statistical assumptions questionable.
%[text] - **Data errors**, such as impossible values, coding mistakes, or duplicated observations. \
%[text] The types of plots we will generally use are as follows:
%[text] - **Histogram:** What does the distribution look like?
%[text] - **Box plot:** How do the groups compare in center and spread?
%[text] - **Swarm plot:** What are the actual observations?
%[text] - **Violin plot:** Where are observations most densely concentrated? \
%%
%[text] ### Histograms
%[text] Histograms are always a great place to start. They are easy to plot and give you a good visualization of the distribution of data. Remember, our Height variable contains the heights for all the children, both male and female. We know that male and female heights have different distributions, so let's plot those separately.
%[text] Try this Prompt:
%[text] ```
%[text] Plot Male and Female heights as overlaid histograms in the same axis. Make the histograms pretty.
%[text] ```
%[text] Copy the copilot code here
%[text] 

%[text] - Notice for a histogram, you first index out the data you want (Male or Female heights). Then you plug the data into the histograms \
%[text] Review:
%[text] - Is the data distributed as expected? Are boys taller than girls?
%[text] - Can you understand the Code?
%[text] - Do you like how the plot turned out? Can you modify the plot? \
%%
%[text] ### Review the distribution
%[text] Is the data normal? Normal means bell-shaped, and if its a normal curve, we can use certain statistics, like t-tests and anovas, to compare the data.
%[text] This normal curve is also known as a Probability Distribution Function, or PDF. The total area under this curve represents a probability of 1. You can break this probability down into parts using the standard deviation and the mean: 
%[text] ![](text:image:117e)
%[text] The Mean establishes the center of the curve and the Standard Deviation the width of the curve
%[text] - 68% (dark blue) of all data fall within one standard deviation (𝜎) of the mean (µ) .
%[text] - 95.45% (blue) of the data falls within 2 standard deviations.
%[text] - 99.73% (light blue) fall within 3 standard deviations \
%[text] Try this prompt:
%[text] How do I determine if the heights are normal?
%[text] 

%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_232a] - Is the data normal? \
%%
%[text] ### Other distribution plots
%[text] While useful, `histograms` can take a lot space and are not especially great for comparing several groups. Histograms also bin the data, which depending on the bin size, can affect the appearance of the histogram. Because of the binning, histograms are not that great for small sample sizes or examining fine details.
%[text] So, let's examine the other types of plots. Try the following prompt:
%[text] - I would like to compare the distribution of male and female heights in 3 horizontal tiles. In the first tile, plot the heights as box charts, in the second as swarm charts, and in the third as violin plots \
%[text] Paste your code here

%[text] - **Box Plot** - shows the location of the median (middle line), the interquartile range (the rectangle), and the spread (the whiskers). The circles are the outliers.
%[text] - **Swarm Chart** - here we see every single data point. The shape of the swarm is determined by frequency (same formula used to generate a Normal PDF)
%[text] - **Violin plot** - overall density and distribution shape. Hides individual points \
%[text] Notice:
%[text] - We can input grouping variables into the functions (`x` is set to Sex), so we don't need to index out the rows we want to plot or use hold to overlay the plots like in histograms.
%[text] - The data is plotted along the y-axis.
%[text] - Notice that the shapes of these plots are fairly symmetrical — this indicates that the data is normal (as we already determined) \
%%
%[text] ### Scatter Plots
%[text] Scatter plots are two-dimensional plots most often used to compare two variables to see if the variables correlate in any fashion. Here we plot Father's heights vs Son's heights. The Father's height should be on the x-axis, since it's the independent variable.
figure %[text:anchor:T_DBD01520]

la = T.Sex == "M"; % mask sons' heights 
x = T.Father(la); % get Father's height
y = T.Height(la); % get Son's heights

hs = scatter(x,y,"filled","MarkerFaceAlpha",0.25); % create scatter plot


xlabel('Father''s Height (in)') % label the x-axis
ylabel("Son's Height (in)") % label the y-axis

xlim([60 80]) % set the x-limits
ylim([60 80]) % set the y-limits
%[text] Notes:
%[text] - Since everything is rounded to the nearest half-inch, there are a lot of dots that overlap
%[text] - Father's height is repeated often, since many fathers have more than one son, giving the scatter plot that columnar appearance
%[text] - Do Father's Height predict Son's heights? We'll discuss further below \
%%
%[text] ## Data Analysis
%[text] The function `summary` nice summarizes the data in the table
summary(T)
%%
%[text] ### Group stats
%[text] Since the data is normal, it makes sense to report the mean and the standard deviation of the data. We can do that for the children's heights as follows:
S = groupsummary(T,"Sex",["mean" "std"],"Height")
%[text] - And we get a nice table of the heights of the children \
%%
%[text] ### Plot as Bar plots
%[text] Mean and standard deviations are often plotted as bar plots with error bars. But coding such bar plots can be unreasonably complicated in MATLAB. Luckily we have Copilot to help. Try this prompt:
%[text] - Plot as bar plots the `mean_Height` in `S`. Overlay the `std_Height` as error bars. Make the plot pretty \

%[text] - If you want different color bars, you can ask: Is there a way to make each bar a different color in the plot? \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Testing for Significance
%[text] Are the Heights Significantly Different? You would expect so, since these are Male vs Female heights. But, how do we test this statistically?
%[text] 
%[text] #### Quick and Dirty Box Plot Method
%[text] - You can actually use box plots to visually determine if the populations are significantly different.
%[text] - You just need to turn on `Notch` \
%[text] ![](text:image:0346)
%[text] - Box charts whose notches do not overlap have different medians at the 5% significance level. \
%%
%[text] Here's the box plot code, but with the notches turned on:
% Common data
figure
x = T.Sex; % Grouping Variable
y = T.Height; % Measured data

% Box chart
boxchart(x, y, 'BoxFaceColor', [0.20 0.45 0.85], 'BoxFaceAlpha', 0.35, 'Notch','on')
box on
title('Box Chart')
xlabel('Sex')
ylabel('Height (in)')
%[text] - Since the notches don't overlap, the medians are different at the 5% significance level.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D157F2F1] ### Hypothesis testing
%[text] How do you calculate the probability that the means are different? Since our distributions are normal, we can use a t-test, which tells us the probability of our means being different.
%[text] Since our data are roughly normal, we can use a t-test:
%[text] - **Null Hypothesis**: Male and Female heights are the same
%[text] - **Alternate Hypothesis**: Male and Female heights are different \
%[text] For two samples (Male vs Female heights), we perform a t-test using the function `ttest2` , as follows
x = T.Height(T.Sex=="M"); % get boys heights
y = T.Height(T.Sex=="F"); % get female heights
[h,p,ci,stats] = ttest2(x,y) % perform t-test
%[text] - h=1 means we reject the null hypothesis
%[text] - Since P is less than 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis \
%%
%[text] Use APA style to report the results. 
%[text] - t-tests described [here](https://www.socscistatistics.com/tutorials/ttest/default.aspx). \
s = sprintf('Males were significantly taller than females, ');
s = sprintf('%s%1.2f ± %1.2f" vs %1.2f ± %1.2f" (M±SD), respectively,\n', s, mean(x), std(x),mean(y), std(y));
s = sprintf('%st(%d) = %1.2f, p < .0001.', s,stats.df, stats.tstat);
disp(s)
%%
%[text] ### Scatter Plots and Correlations
%[text] So, do Fathers' Heights predict Son's heights?
%[text] Here is the scatter plot again
figure %[text:anchor:T_DBD01520]

la = T.Sex == "M"; % mask sons' heights 
x = T.Father(la); % get Father's height
y = T.Height(la); % get Son's heights

hs = scatter(x,y,"filled","MarkerFaceAlpha",0.25); % create scatter plot


xlabel('Father''s Height (in)') % label the x-axis
ylabel("Son's Height (in)") % label the y-axis

xlim([60 80]) % set the x-limits
ylim([60 80]) % set the y-limits
grid on
%[text] - The question can boiled down to: Given the Father's Height, what is the Son's Height?
%[text] - Review some of the outlier plot scatter points in th plot. How close is the son's height to his father's? \
%%
%[text] #### Equality Line
%[text] If the Father's Height exactly predicted the son's height, then it would be a straight line that looks something like this:
hold on % need to hold to not overwrite the scatter plot
plot([60 80],[60 80],':k','DisplayName','Equality'); % the 45-degree equality line
%[text] - The line represents exact equality between son and father's heights. i.e. 60" father has a son of exactly 60" and so on
%[text] - Seems to fit the data pretty well, is it the best fit line for the data? \
%%
%[text] ### Best Fit Line
%[text] Linear regression tells us the likely Y value, given X. Linear regression creates a **best-fitting straight line** through the data points that you can use to predict the Y from the X (The son's height from the father's). 
%[text] We calculate linear regression using the functions `polyfit` and `polyval` as shown below. (Copilot Prompt: how do you calculate a linear regression line from x and y vector data?)
% calculate the regression line
p = polyfit(x, y, 1);  % creates best fit line (returns slope and intercept)
xFit = linspace(min(x), max(x), 100); % create a vector of x values from min to max of data
yFit = polyval(p, xFit); % calculate Y values from x using best-fit line

% plot the regression line
hold on
plot(xFit, yFit, 'k-', 'LineWidth', 1, 'DisplayName', 'Best Fit Line')
%[text] - The dotted line is the line of equality — a perfect correlation of sons' and fathers' heights
%[text] - The solid line is the regression line — a "best-fit" line that shows the actual correlation between father's and son's heights
%[text] - The best-fit line suggests that Tall fathers have sons that are slightly shorter than them and vice versa \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Calculate the correlation
%[text] Correlation tells us how well X and Y track together linearly (How Similar are the Son's Heights to the Father's Heights). You can think of correlation as kind of like the slope of the regression line, but in a unit-less fashion, so correlation (or r) always falls between -1 and 1. The closer to one of these extremes the stronger X and Y move together
%[text] The function `corr` calculates the correlation between data
father_son_r = corr(x,y);
title(sprintf('Father Son Height corr = %1.2f',father_son_r))
%[text] - A positive correlation means that the relationship between the two variables moves in the same direction (taller son -\> taller father)
%[text] - 0.39 means the heights are clearly correlated \
%%
%[text] What about the correlations for all the other relationships?
%[text] - Father-Daughter
%[text] - Mother-Son
%[text] - Mother-Daughter \
%[text] We could just repeat the code above, replacing `x` and `y` with the corresponding data OR we can leverage the power of `groupsummary`
%[text] This function call calculates the correlations for Father vs Son and Father vs Daughter
R = groupsummary(T,"Sex",@corr,{"Father","Height"})
%[text] Here we use
%[text] -  "Sex" as the Grouping Variable
%[text] -  `@corr` as the stat to perform. The at sign (@) means function handle (kind of like a path to the function)
%[text] - Father and Height as the variables to calculate the stats on. Since `corr` requires 2 inputs, we need to provide the names of two of the table columns. They also need to be cell arrays.  \
%%
%[text] If we want to calculate all the correlations, the group call would look like this:
R = groupsummary(T,"Sex",@corr,{["Mother","Father"], ["Height","Height"]})
%%
%[text] Since we used a function handle, groupsummary doesn't know what stat was performed, so it just entered 'fun1'. We can easily replace 'fun1' in the column headers as follows: 
R.Properties.VariableNames = replace(R.Properties.VariableNames,'fun1','corr')
%%
%[text] ## Tiled Plots
%[text] How would we plot all permutations of the various parent-child correlations?
%[text] - e.g. Father-Son, Father-daughter, Mother-Son, Mother-daughter? \
%[text] I have packaged the scatter and regression plotting functions into a function called [plot\_height\_corr](internal:M_C6A13C97). Let's review that function now
%[text] Now that we have a nice function, we can plot all 4 Comparisons. 
figure
nexttile
plot_height_corr(T,"Father","F")
%%
%[text] - Add the other 3 comparisons \
nexttile
plot_height_corr(T,"Father","M")
%%
nexttile
plot_height_corr(T,"Mother","M")
%%
nexttile
plot_height_corr(T,"Mother","F")
%%
%[text] ### Analyzing Parents Height
%[text] What if we want to calculate the average parents' heights? Calculating the mean parents' height is a little trickier since the data for each parent is repeated multiple times. The trick here is to realize that the stat function `mode` will return the most common height for a given set of heights. So then this just becomes a simple `groupsummary` function call where we use "Family" as the grouping variable
pT = groupsummary(T,"Family","mode",["Father" "Mother"]) % create parent table of heights
%[text] - now, we have just the one height per Father and Mother
%[text] - Notice there are less rows in this table, than in T
%[text] - The Number of rows equals the number of families in the dataset (one set of parents per family) \
%[text] And we can replace 'mode\_' with 'height\_' to make the variable names more clear
pT.Properties.VariableNames = replace(pT.Properties.VariableNames,'mode_','height_')
%[text] 
%[text] 
%%
%[text] #### Visualize Parents Heights Distributions
%[text] What type of plot should we use to visualize the distribution of heights?

%%
%[text] #### Compare parents heights
%[text] So, are the parent's heights assortative? Do tall fathers marry tall mothers (and vice versa)?
%[text] What should we plot to visualize the relationship? And what should we calculate?

%%
%[text] `We can use groupsummary` to calculate the mean and std heights of the parents. Here, though, we don't need a grouping variable because we want to calculate the stats for all of the data in each column. So, we enter the empty brackets for the grouping variable, as follows:
pS = groupsummary(pT,[],["mean" "std"],["height_Father" "height_Mother"]) % parent stats
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C793FB72] ### Visualize Parent's mean height as bar plots
%[text] - We don't have a grouping variable, we just have two columns in `pS`
%[text] - Bar plot accepts vectors, so we can just pull the data as a vector using curly bracket indexing \
figure
x = categorical(["F" "M"]); 
y = pS{1,["mean_height_Mother" "mean_height_Father"]}; % curly bracket indexing pulls out mean data as a numeric vector
bar(x,y,FaceAlpha=0.5) % bar plot

hold on % overlay

e = pS{1,["std_height_Mother" "std_height_Father"]};  % curly bracket indexing pulls out std data as a numeric vector
errorbar(x,y,e,'k',LineStyle='none')
ylabel("Parents' Heights")
%[text] - Notice that a bar plot is the visualization of just 2 data points: the mean and standard deviation
%[text] - Compare to a histogram or boxchart, which is a visualization of all the datapoints.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_BE801CA7] # Local Functions
function plot_height_corr(T,parent,sex) %[text:anchor:M_C6A13C97]
% INPUTS
    % parent: "Father" or "Mother"
    % sex: "M" or "F"


% get property names
props = table(["F";"M"],["Daughter"; "Son"],["magenta"; "blue"], VariableNames=["sex","child","color"]); % property name table
child = props.child(props.sex==sex); % used in ylabel
color = props.color(props.sex==sex); % used to set color of scatter plot 

% get a smaller table with just selected rows
t = T(T.Sex==sex,:); 
x = t.(parent);
y = t.Height;

% scatter plot
scatter(x,y,color,"filled","MarkerFaceAlpha",0.25);

xlabel(sprintf("%s's Height (in)",parent)) % label the x-axis
ylabel(sprintf("%s's Height (in)",child)) % label the y-axis

xlim([50 80]) % set the x-limits
ylim([50 80]) % set the y-limits

% equality line
hold on % need to hold to not overwrite the scatter plot
plot([50 80],[50 80],':k','DisplayName','Equality');  

% regression line
p = polyfit(x, y, 1);
xFit = linspace(min(x), max(x), 100);
yFit = polyval(p, xFit);
plot(xFit, yFit, 'k-', 'LineWidth', 1, 'DisplayName', 'Best Fit Line')

% calculate the correlation
c = corr(x,y);
title(sprintf('corr = %1.2f',c))

end
%[text] import table
function T = getGaltonData(filename, dataLines) %[text:anchor:M_8bd5]
%GETGALTONDATA Import data from a csv file. Created using the MATLAB
%Import Tool
%  T = IMPORTFILE(FILENAME) reads data from text file FILENAME for the
%  default selection.  Returns the data as a table.
%
%  T = IMPORTFILE(FILE, DATALINES) reads data for the specified row
%  interval(s) of text file FILENAME. Specify DATALINES as a positive
%  scalar integer or a N-by-2 array of positive scalar integers for
%  dis-contiguous row intervals.
%
%  Example:
%  T = importfile("/Users/ernesto/MATLAB-Drive/MtMresources/data/unit1/05-1-galton-x.csv", [2, Inf]);
%
%  See also READTABLE.
%
% Auto-generated by MATLAB on 06-Sep-2023 19:15:23

%%\ Input handling

% If dataLines is not specified, define defaults
if nargin < 2
    dataLines = [2, Inf];
end

%%\ Set up the Import Options and import the data
opts = delimitedTextImportOptions("NumVariables", 6);

% Specify range and delimiter
opts.DataLines = dataLines;
opts.Delimiter = ",";

% Specify column names and types
opts.VariableNames = ["Family", "Father", "Mother", "Sex", "Height", "Kids"];
opts.VariableTypes = ["categorical", "double", "double", "categorical", "double", "double"];

% Specify file level properties
opts.ExtraColumnsRule = "ignore";
opts.EmptyLineRule = "read";

% Specify variable properties
opts = setvaropts(opts, ["Family", "Sex"], "EmptyFieldRule", "auto");

% Import the data
T = readtable(filename, opts);

end

